1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
AI agents need data, but every API wants
an account, a subscription and a key.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Agents can't sign up,
and you don't want to hand them your card.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,000
BFA is an API market built for agents.
You sign in with Google.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Your browser creates a NEAR key
that never leaves it.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:45,000
You sign two transactions yourself: a vault with your daily limit,
and a gateway key that can only call sign_eip3009.

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:55,000
To add funds, you send USDC
to your own top-up address.

7
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:05,000
A public contract splits it: $1.00 to your vault, $0.015 to BFA.
We never hold your money.

8
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Each agent gets its own API key, with an expiry date,
a daily cap, per-API caps and an allowlist.

9
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Now the agent works on its own. Claude Code finds the API,
reads the exact price, and pays for one call.

10
00:01:40,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Every call has a receipt. The payment comes from your vault,
signed by NEAR Chain Signatures and checked on NEAR.

11
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:15,000
When the agent hits its limit, the next call is refused
before anything is paid. Failed and empty calls are free.

12
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Anyone can list an API by pull request; we pin the manifest hash.
BFA is open source and live on testnet today.

13
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
BFA. Built for agents.
